 can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"No additional core force needs to be posited beyond the avowal. The competitive and reward-bearing setting may explain the declaration’s strategic purpose, but answering Lear’s question and seeking favourable evaluation do not constitute a distinct core mode of the focal words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most, explicitly connecting their answers with his bounty. Called upon first, Goneril delivers the focal avowal as the opening of an elaborate claim that her love exceeds ordinary valuation and expression.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no specific undertaking by Goneril. Lear accepts her declaration favourably and immediately grants her and Albany’s issue a large, perpetual territorial share, but that is uptake of her professed love within his contest, not uptake of a promise. The later supplied evidence attributes serious harms to Lear’s two elder daughters, creating reason to doubt the declaration’s sincerity, but it does not conclusively settle Goneril’s exact feelings at the moment of speaking.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, including the opening love-test scene.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly establishes the utterance’s avowing form and its public, reward-linked setting. It does not conclusively establish Goneril’s subjective emotional state at that exact moment, nor does later wrongdoing alone prove that she then felt no affection.\"}"
        }
